nothing  moving on now and the son of libya's  former leader muammar gaddafi has  registered as a candidate for december's  presidential elections saif al-islam  gaddafi said he wants to restore unity  after a decade of conflict since his  father's downfall the 49 year old is  wanted by the international criminal  court for war crimes ibrahim freihart is  an associate professor of conflict  resolution at the doha institute and  joins us live now thanks for joining us  uh ibrahim first of all this is a man  that was supposed to be executed at one  point and now he's registering as a  presidential candidate it certainly is a  significant turnaround will he have any  support  well he definitely benefited from the  chaos uh the security uh situation  that's in disarray in libya and that uh  ended up actually in uh releasing him in  2017  from prison though he had uh he received  a death sentence  in 2015 and he is still wanted by the  international criminal court now whether  he has support he does actually have  some support not a lot he has some  support among the former regime  loyalists  and also he has some support within some  certain tribal forces  they're not much uh  it's not a strong support they are in  places like uh benny waleed and tarhona  and and some other tribal forces  i don't think he has any chance of  winning this election i don't think he  uh he himself thinks that he has a  chance but for him i think this is a  political message that he is trying to  send that he is becoming uh again back  to the political scene and part of the  game and also that he can run for the  elections and he  he  is  ignoring the international criminal  court's uh  uh request for  for him to be handed over to the to the  icc so that's i think the political  message is what we're seeing behind it  and uh he is doing it uh now  as we see  yeah that was my next question ibrahim  given he's wanted for crimes against  humanity is is gaddafi even eligible to  run  well there is uh again as i said there  is a political uh chaos in libya now uh  there are no laws there are no rules  that are governing this uh whether this  elections or any other things as we know  libya is uh sharply and  sharply divided  polarized unfortunately among the  different political forces and there are  dozens of different political forces so  legality and law and uh  it's not the major driving force uh  that's regulating these elections or any  other things  because it depends where you know  what area are we talking about  uh we know that it's deeply divided  between east and west but we we are  seeing syphilis  actually is running from the south from  sabha so it's completely outside  this  area within the east and west equation  so he's coming with uh with a different  uh from a different area but for the icc  i think the request uh will still be  there because uh this is serious and  it's it's official  that they wanted uh  islamic gaddafi for war was against uh  uh  the the protesters in in libya during uh  his father's time so i think this  situation will continue and as in again  as i said  i don't think he has a serious chance  but it's the political message it's  what's driving his uh running for this  election  ibrahim how likely is it that these  elections on december 24th will even go  ahead given the disagreements over the  rules and the schedule of this poll  well in libya we learned that nothing  will happen until it happens and yes all  choices all scenarios are  out there on the table it depends on how  there are there there isn't one  decision-making process that uh is  taking place in in libya uh we know that  there are many uh that they're running  for this election uh  and several others that are running for  uh this election so  it all depends on how uh  how things go and whether these people  different political forces remain  committed uh to the political process so  it's a larger it's a part of a larger  issue  about the holy transition  issue the whole transitional process in  libya as long as there is a commitment  from the  domestic as well as the international  players in in libya then yes we can  expect that the elections will happen  but as we know again uh there are  different parties from within libya and  also internationally even the european  position is divided among like parties  like france and italy and several others  so uh i think it will be the outcome of  all these uh  of all this polarization within libya  whether we will see it for for sure  happening uh  in the proper time  well it's only a couple of weeks away so  we will watch and see thank you very  much ibrahim frahat in doha there

